A good number of --jobs could perhaps be determined automically, by
starting low (eg 2) and working up (to eg numcpus), finding the optimal
number of jobs along the way, and settling down to that number (plus or
minus one to detect when the optimal number changes).

This needs a way to measure performance. The ways to do that measurement
would vary depending on the UsedStages set used by the command. 

For transferStages, most files will involve a transfer with a progress bar.
Only measure those. Measure the average total throughput of all running
transfers. But also measure the time spend processing each file before and
after the transfer progress bar. It may be that increasing the number of
jobs does not increase total throughput, but that the overhead of other
processing is able to be parallelized. In that case, it's still worth
changing the number of jobs.

For commandStages, measure how long it takes to process a file
on average, divided by the number of jobs.

How well that works will depend a lot of what the command does.
Eg, a `git-annex drop` is often doing the close the same amount
of work per file, so this would work well. A `git-annex add`
is not, so this wouldn't. It might be worth moving commands
like that to use something other than commandStages that can be measured
in a different way. (Eg for `git-annex add`, use the progress bar to
measure the average total throughput.)

transferStages is not currently used for most uploads, so for commands
that upload, there would need to be a new UsedStages set that
is like `commandStages` but that causes throughput to be measured.
(Similar to the one needed for `git-annex add`.)

Commands could also hint at what a good optimal value is likely to be.
Eg, for `git-annex drop`, it's almost always good to have a decently
high number of jobs, because locking is latency bound and parallelizes very
well. So it would make sense to start near the middle of the range,
rather than near the bottom.
--[[Joey]]
